His saying, ﴿Indeed, We will send the she-camel as a trial for them﴾.

His saying, ﴿We will send the she-camel﴾, means: We will bring her out of the rock (hadabah). ﴿As a trial for them﴾ means as a test and an examination. It is a *maf'ul li-ajlihi* (object of purpose, the accusative of cause or reason), because they had proposed to Salih that he bring out for them a she-camel from a rock, and that if she came out for them from it, they would believe in him and follow him. So Allah brought the she-camel out of that rock as a miracle for Salih, and as a trial and examination for them. That she-camel was a miracle they witnessed with their own eyes, and Allah warned them, through the tongue of His Prophet Salih, not to touch her with harm, and that if they meddled with her by any harm, Allah would seize them with His punishment.

The commentators say that they said to him: "If you bring out for us from this rock a she-camel that is pregnant, ten months gone, and of a great size (wabra' 'ushara'), we will follow you."

What this noble verse indicates, that Allah sent her to them as a test and an examination, and that if they assailed this sign of Allah, which is the she-camel, with harm, He would destroy them, has been made clear in other verses of the Book of Allah:

- As His saying in Surat al-A'raf: (p-480) ﴿There has certainly come to you clear evidence from your Lord. This is the she-camel of Allah, a sign for you. So leave her to eat within the land of Allah, and do not touch her with harm, lest a painful punishment seize you﴾ [al-A'raf: 73];

- And His saying in Surat Hud, concerning Salih: ﴿And O my people, this is the she-camel of Allah, a sign for you. So leave her to eat within the land of Allah, and do not touch her with harm, lest a near punishment seize you.﴾ ﴿But they hamstrung her, so he said, "Enjoy yourselves in your homes for three days. That is a promise not to be denied."﴾ [Hud: 64–65];

- And His saying in Surat ash-Shu'ara': ﴿He said, "This is a she-camel; for her is a drinking turn, and for you is a drinking turn on a known day."﴾ ﴿And do not touch her with harm, lest the punishment of a tremendous day seize you.﴾ [ash-Shu'ara': 155–156].

Allah has also made clear that they hamstrung the she-camel, and then the all-destroying punishment came upon them, in verses of His Book:

- As His saying in al-A'raf: ﴿So they hamstrung the she-camel and defied the command of their Lord﴾, up to His saying: ﴿So the earthquake seized them, and they became in their home prostrate corpses﴾ [al-A'raf: 77–78];

- And His saying: ﴿So they hamstrung her, and in the morning they became regretful.﴾ ﴿Then the punishment seized them﴾ [ash-Shu'ara': 157–158];

- And His saying: ﴿So they denied him and hamstrung her, and their Lord destroyed them over their sin, leveling them﴾, the verse [ash-Shams: 14].

We have explained this with the utmost clarity in Surat Fussilat, in our discussion of His saying: ﴿So there seized them the punishment of the humiliating thunderbolt for what they used to earn﴾, the verse [Fussilat: 17].
